INT. NEONATAL ICU - NIGHT

Dim blue light. Monitors pulse. Incubators glow like small aquariums. A mobile of paper cranes turns slowly.

DR. THEODORA ASHWORTH-OKORO (40s, scrubs, hair pinned with a pencil) stands at an incubator, scribbling on a chart. NURSE BEATRICE LINDQVIST-ADEYEMI (50s, soft shoes, a pen behind her ear) squats beside her, adjusting a monitor lead.

THEODORA
His oxygen's dropping.

BEATRICE
I see it.

THEODORA
Turn the flow up.

Beatrice reaches for the dial. Turns it. Nothing changes. She turns it again.

BEATRICE
It's stuck.

THEODORA
Move.

She crouches, pushes the dial, finds the line kinked beneath the incubator. Straightens it. The monitor beeps higher.

THEODORA (CONT'D)
(exhaling)
There.

BEATRICE
You were fast.

THEODORA
You were faster. If you hadn't called it...

BEATRICE
Don't say it.

THEODORA
I'm not going to.

They stand over the incubator, watching the tiny chest rise.

BEATRICE
She was a month early.

THEODORA
She's strong.

BEATRICE
She is.

Beatrice's hand drifts to the incubator's edge. She rests a finger on the clear plastic.

BEATRICE (CONT'D)
My daughter was this size once.

THEODORA
How is she now?

BEATRICE
Thirty. Never calls.

THEODORA
She will.

BEATRICE
Will she?

THEODORA
When she has a baby of her own.

Beatrice laughs, softly, then wipes her eyes. She straightens up. She picks up the chart and hands it to Theodora.

BEATRICE
Doctor. You forgot to sign.

THEODORA
I know.

She takes the pen from behind Beatrice's ear, signs, and tucks it back. Behind them, the mobile of cranes turns slowly in the blue light.
